{
  "id": "T18_49_the_interpretation_in_force_is_ambiguous_only_when_normalizations_that_apply_differ",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.3.8",
    "4.6.1",
    "4.5.3",
    "7.6.6",
    "9.6.1",
    "5.5.4",
    "9.4.2"
  ],
  "description": "The project is normalized under the fixture fire-rating interpretation and wing B, inside it, under a fixture set interpretation. A read scoped to the project resolves to fire-rating and names the project's normalization; one scoped to wing B, which is a member of both scopes, resolves to an ambiguous interpretation in force listing both normalizations, except at a cutoff before wing B's own normalization took effect. Two normalizations of zone C at one instant that name the same interpretation are not ambiguous: the frame cites both, in identifier order. Two of zone D at one instant that name different interpretations are ambiguous. A frame that names an interpretation carries no normalization member. Door 170 belongs to zones E and F, normalized at different times to the same interpretation: both put it in force, so a comparison that matches only under it cites both in matched_by, and the resolved frame cites both.",
  "note": "7.3.8 *Interpretation in force*: \"When several of its normalizations are tied or unordered at t, with no `@after` evidence ordering them (4.5.2), and they name different interpretations, it is ambiguous (4.5.3); when they name the same one, that one is in force ... it is ambiguous, listing the normalizations concerned, when they differ ... Where a rule cites the normalization that put an interpretation in force, or the one in force, and several did, it cites all of them, as a list in the order of 7.6.6.\" README section 5, Frame: `normalization` is `{\"ambiguous\": [refs]}` \"listing the normalizations concerned, sorted\", and \"is absent when the frame names an interpretation\"; Normalizations: \"the `normalization` of a resolved frame ... are the list of their compact references, ordered as in 7.6.6\". 7.3.8: \"The normalizations that *put* an interpretation in force for an Item at t are, for each such scope Item, its latest normalization effective at or before t, or each of its tied latest ones\"; README section 5, Normalizations: `matched_by` and `normalization` are then \"the list of their compact references, ordered as in 7.6.6\".",
